(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["principles-of-diagnostic-medicine-i-exam-1-pdm-exam-1-master-exam-form-c"]={"title":"PDM I Exam 1 Master Exam — Form C","category":"Principles of Diagnostic Medicine I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"How negative must middle-ear pressure be before it is considered significant for treatment?","choices":["Any negative pressure at all","More negative than minus two hundred millimetres of water","More negative than minus fifty millimetres of water","More negative than minus one hundred millimetres of water"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — that is the threshold. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 32 (speaker notes)"},{"q":"Why do acanthocytes form?","choices":["From loss of surface membrane during splenic passage","From denaturation of hemoglobin within the cell","From abnormalities in lipid metabolism or alterations in the red cell membrane","From mechanical shearing as the cells pass through fibrin strands in vessels"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — abnormalities in lipid metabolism, or alcohol. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 36 (speaker notes)"},{"q":"Which findings does orbital computed tomography show?","choices":["Cystic versus solid character, size and vascularity","Orbital cellulitis with fat stranding, abscess, blowout fracture and herniated orbital contents","Mucosal thickening, air-fluid levels and complete sinus opacification on the affected side","Rim-enhancing fluid collection and necrotic lymph nodes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the orbital column as listed. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A state enacts laboratory regulation that differs from the federal Clinical Laboratory Improvement Amendments. What is the relationship?","choices":["State and city regulation will always be stricter, because the federal standard is the minimum and cannot be downgraded","State regulation supersedes the federal standard in either direction, so a state may relax the requirement if it chooses","Federal regulation applies only where no state rule exists","State regulation applies only to hospital laboratories"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — states may add requirements but may never relax them. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A patient receiving intravenous fluids containing dextrose has glucose in the urine. How is that read?","choices":["It confirms undiagnosed diabetes mellitus","It is expected in a patient receiving dextrose","It indicates the fluids should be stopped","It indicates renal tubular damage"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — this is a recognised and normal explanation. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What causes a cholestatic pattern?","choices":["Heart failure and sepsis","Bile duct obstruction, gallstones, and primary biliary cholangitis","Viral hepatitis, alcohol, ischaemia, and drug injury","Gilbert syndrome and haemolysis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — those three. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 17 (speaker notes)"},{"q":"Which agency issues laboratory certificates and conducts inspections?","choices":["The Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services","The Food and Drug Administration","The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention","The National Institutes of Health"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it also collects user fees, approves accreditation organizations and publishes the rules. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What are the midsagittal and parasagittal planes?","choices":["Midsagittal runs across the shoulders; parasagittal sections lie anterior and posterior","Midsagittal runs at the level of the heart; parasagittal sections are angled off the axis","Midsagittal runs in the body's midline; parasagittal sections lie to either side of it","Midsagittal runs through the umbilicus; parasagittal sections lie above and below it"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the midsagittal plane is also called the median plane. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which distinction does audiometry make?","choices":["Conductive from sensorineural hearing loss","Effusion from perforation","Acute from chronic otitis media with effusion","Unilateral from bilateral loss"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the type of loss, not merely its degree. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A macrocytic anemia shows NO megaloblastic changes on the smear. Which two diagnoses follow?","choices":["Hemolysis and acute hemorrhage","Chronic liver disease and acute hematologic malignancy","Vitamin B12 deficiency and dietary folate deficiency","Iron deficiency and thalassemia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the two arms on the no-megaloblastic-change branch. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"What is the risk of diagnosing a fungal infection clinically alone?","choices":["The wrong antifungal class is chosen","Resistance develops to topical antifungals","Fungal infections can be misidentified","Treatment is delayed while the culture is awaited"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — which is why microscopy confirms it. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What must be assessed before a magnetic resonance study with contrast, and why?","choices":["Thyroid function, because gadolinium chelates iodine and can precipitate a thyroid storm","Nothing beforehand, because gadolinium is inert and is excreted unchanged in the bile","Blood urea nitrogen and creatinine, because renal function matters primarily for clearance","Blood urea nitrogen and creatinine, because gadolinium is more nephrotoxic than iodine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — gadolinium can damage kidneys, though less than iodinated contrast. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"On a bone scan, why are the kidneys visible?","choices":["Uptake in the kidneys indicates the tracer dose given was higher than the study required","Uptake in the kidneys suggests metastatic disease has reached the retroperitoneal organs","Uptake in the kidneys occurs only when renal function is impaired and clearance is slowed","Uptake in the kidneys is normal, and the same is true of tracer on positron emission scans"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — renal uptake is normal on both studies. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What is the neutrophil's role?","choices":["The body's main defense against bacteria and its primary defense against microbial invasion, by phagocytosis","The body's main defense against parasites and its primary defense against allergens, by releasing granule contents","Antibody production and cellular immune response","Immune surveillance and differentiation into macrophages"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the main defense against bacteria, by phagocytosis. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"In which cell is hyperchromia observed, and what are its index values?","choices":["Echinocytes, with mean corpuscular hemoglobin above thirty-three picograms","Spherocytes, with mean corpuscular hemoglobin above thirty-three picograms and concentration above thirty-six grams per decilitre","Target cells, with mean corpuscular hemoglobin above thirty-three picograms","Schistocytes, with mean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration above thirty-six grams per decilitre of packed cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — which is why the concentration flags hereditary spherocytosis. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What mean corpuscular volume defines macrocytosis, and what causes it?","choices":["Greater than one hundred femtolitres, from iron deficiency or thalassemia","Greater than eighty femtolitres, from vitamin B12 deficiency","Greater than one hundred femtolitres, from vitamin B12 or folate deficiency","Less than eighty femtolitres, from vitamin B12 or folate deficiency"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the threshold and the two examples given. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Specificity relates to fewer of which kind of error?","choices":["False positives","False negatives","Both false positives and false negatives equally","Neither; specificity addresses precision rather than error type"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and specificity does not address false negatives. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What does a punch biopsy provide, and what is it used for?","choices":["A sample of the entire lesion, used for pigmented lesions of any size","A full-thickness sample, used for inflammatory rashes and small lesions","A full-thickness sample, used only when melanoma is suspected","A superficial sample, used for raised epidermal lesions"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — both halves. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A patient on anticoagulation reports blood in the urine. What should that raise?","choices":["The possibility of a reduced tubular threshold","The possibility of rhabdomyolysis","Over-aggressive anticoagulation","The possibility of intravascular hemolysis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — excessive anticoagulation is a recognised cause. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Persistent proteinuria is best understood as what?","choices":["A normal variant in most adults","A significant sign of renal disease","Evidence of urinary tract infection","Proof of nephrotic syndrome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — persistence is what makes it significant. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which syndrome shows liver and kidney failure at once?","choices":["Hepatorenal syndrome","Cardiorenal syndrome","Diabetic ketoacidosis","Nephrotic syndrome"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — hepatorenal syndrome. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"How should blood urea nitrogen be read?","choices":["Interpret it alongside creatinine, not alone","Interpret it alone, since creatinine is less specific","Interpret it only when the patient is fasting","Interpret it only if the estimated filtration rate is normal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — alongside creatinine, not alone. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 14 (speaker notes)"},{"q":"Which ophthalmologic emergency is an indication for tonometry?","choices":["Orbital cellulitis","Corneal ulcer","Acute angle-closure glaucoma","Chronic open-angle glaucoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — named as an ophthalmologic emergency. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What is the most important thing to remember about ultrasound orientation?","choices":["Where the indicator is: on the left of the screen in cardiac imaging, on the right for everything else","How deep the focus is set: shallow for superficial structures, deep for abdominal structures","Which frequency is selected: high for superficial structures, low for deeper structures","Where the indicator is: on the right of the screen in cardiac imaging, on the left for everything else"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it is called crucial, because the whole image flips with it. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"How is radiation dose defined and expressed?","choices":["Ionizing energy delivered per exposure, in millisieverts only, where one millisievert is one milliwatt","Ionizing energy absorbed per unit of mass, in grays or milligrays, where one gray is one joule per kilogram","Ionizing energy emitted per unit of time, in grays or milligrays, where one gray is one joule per second","Ionizing energy absorbed per unit of volume, in sieverts only, where one sievert is one joule per litre"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — and equivalent dose is then expressed in sieverts or millisieverts. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What is the difference between hemoglobin and hematocrit?","choices":["Hemoglobin is the percentage of blood which is red cells; hematocrit is the amount of hemoglobin per volume","Hemoglobin is the number of red cells per volume; hematocrit is their average size","Hemoglobin is measured directly; hematocrit is a marker of oxygen saturation","Hemoglobin is the amount of hemoglobin in a volume of blood; hematocrit is the percentage of that blood which is red cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — one is a concentration of protein, the other a proportion of volume. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which cell has a large spherical nucleus with a thin rim of pale blue cytoplasm, and which has a kidney-shaped nucleus with abundant pale blue cytoplasm?","choices":["Lymphocyte spherical, monocyte kidney-shaped","Monocyte spherical, lymphocyte kidney-shaped","Neutrophil spherical, eosinophil kidney-shaped","Basophil spherical, neutrophil kidney-shaped"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the two agranulocytes, from the picture-only morphology figure. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 10 (image only)"},{"q":"Before ordering a test, which two questions should be asked about it?","choices":["Is it available in-house, and how quickly does it result?","Has the patient had it before, and did they tolerate it?","Is it the newest available assay, and is it automated?","Will it support or guide management, and is it cost effective?"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — appropriateness for this patient is the first consideration, not availability. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which principle is stated about documentation in the posttest phase?","choices":["Documentation may be deferred until results are final","Only abnormal results require documentation","Verbal handoff is sufficient when the result is communicated directly","“If it wasn't documented, it wasn't done”"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — detailed documentation is described as essential to accurate record keeping and reporting. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which three tests count as bedside testing?","choices":["Tzanck smear, Gram stain and skin culture","Potassium hydroxide preparation, Tzanck smear and dermoscopy","Potassium hydroxide preparation, Tzanck smear and Gram stain","Potassium hydroxide preparation, Wood lamp and Gram stain"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — those three. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What are the three phases of the diagnostic testing process, in order?","choices":["Collection, transport, interpretation","Pretest, intratest, posttest","Preanalytical, postanalytical, analytical","Screening, diagnostic, confirmatory"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — also called the preanalytical, analytical and postanalytical phases. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Urine is yellow-brown with a green tinge. Which substance does that point to?","choices":["Urea","Bilirubin","Hemoglobin","Myoglobin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a yellow-brown or green colour points to bilirubin. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What increases monocytes, and what decreases them?","choices":["Increased by bacterial infection, burns and steroids; decreased by radiation","Increased by acute bacterial infection only; decreased by viral infection","Increased by chronic inflammation, stress and viral infection; decreased by bone marrow injury","Increased by parasitic infection, allergy and cancer; decreased by bone marrow injury"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — three causes up and one down. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which of these is a recognised cause of hemoglobinuria?","choices":["Electric shock","Over-aggressive anticoagulation","Statin therapy","Severe burns"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — extensive burns destroy red cells intravascularly. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"How is point-of-care testing defined?","choices":["Any laboratory test with a turnaround time under one hour","Testing performed by a licensed clinician rather than by a laboratory technologist or technician","Testing that does not require a laboratory certificate","Medical testing completed outside the centralized laboratory, at or close to the site of patient care"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — also called near-patient, remote, satellite laboratory testing or rapid diagnostics. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Why does knowing the pre-test probability help before a test is ordered?","choices":["It indicates the chance the condition is present beforehand, which helps decide whether the test is worth ordering at all","It allows the sensitivity and specificity of the test to be adjusted for that particular patient before it is ordered","It determines which collection tube should be used","It establishes whether the test requires a laboratory certificate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — that judgement comes from the patient's presentation and risk factors. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Why can magnetic resonance imaging show tissues that are surrounded by bone better than computed tomography can?","choices":["Calcium emits no signal, so the bone does not obscure what sits inside it","Calcium emits a strong signal, so the bone can be subtracted from the image afterwards","Bone is windowed out during acquisition, so only the soft tissue range is displayed","Bone absorbs the radio waves, so the surrounding soft tissue returns a relatively brighter signal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — silence from calcium is an advantage rather than a gap. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Where does creatinine come from?","choices":["It is produced by the kidney tubules during filtration","It is a waste product of muscle creatine metabolism","It is a waste product of protein metabolism produced by the liver","It is filtered from dietary creatine without being metabolised"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a waste product of muscle creatine metabolism. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"How is the anion gap adjusted when albumin is low?","choices":["Correct it downward by about 2.5 for every one gram per decilitre the albumin has fallen","No correction is needed, because albumin is not charged","Correct it by 1.6 for every one gram per decilitre","Correct it upward by about 2.5 for every one gram per decilitre the albumin has fallen"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — upward by about 2.5 for every one gram per decilitre below normal. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Homonymous hemianopia is found. Where is the lesion, and what should be considered?","choices":["Chiasmal, and it queries a pituitary tumour","Prechiasmal, and it queries optic neuritis","Retinal, and it queries a branch artery occlusion","Retrochiasmal, and it queries a stroke"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — retrochiasmal, and stroke has to be considered. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Ordered from the most negative Hounsfield number to the most positive, what is the sequence of tissues?","choices":["Metal, bone, soft tissue, water, fat, air","Fat, air, water, bone, soft tissue, metal","Air, fat, water, soft tissue, bone, metal","Air, water, fat, soft tissue, bone, metal"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — and it runs blackest to whitest in exactly that order. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which four questions frame the choice of an imaging modality?","choices":["Which modality the radiologist prefers, which is available today, which the insurer covers, and which is quickest","Which modality gives the highest resolution, the largest field of view, the thinnest slices, and the fastest overall scan time for the patient","Which modality best rules the diagnosis in or out, is there a lower-radiation alternative, what is the risk against benefit for this patient, and is contrast needed","Which modality is cheapest, which is fastest, which is closest, and which the patient prefers"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — those are the four. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What does positive predictive value indicate?","choices":["The likelihood that a positive test result identifies someone with the disease","The likelihood that the test will be positive when disease is present","The proportion of the population that has the disease","The likelihood that the test will be negative when disease is absent"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — and negative predictive value is the likelihood that a negative result identifies someone without it. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What distinguishes pseudohyponatraemia from a true hypotonic hyponatraemia?","choices":["The urine sodium is above 40 in pseudohyponatraemia","The osmolality is normal in pseudohyponatraemia","The osmolality is low in pseudohyponatraemia","The potassium is also low in pseudohyponatraemia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a falsely low sodium with a normal osmolality. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What is blood urea nitrogen, and where is it produced and cleared?","choices":["A breakdown product of red blood cells, conjugated by the liver","An enzyme released from injured hepatocytes into the blood","A nitrogenous waste product of protein metabolism, produced by the liver and cleared by the kidneys","A nitrogenous waste product of muscle metabolism, produced and cleared by the kidneys"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — its origin and its clearance. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What does a decreased reticulocyte count suggest, and what does an increased one suggest?","choices":["Decreased suggests iron deficiency; increased suggests a vitamin B12 deficiency","Decreased suggests acute blood loss; increased suggests bone marrow failure","Decreased suggests underproduction of red cells; increased suggests hemolysis or blood loss","Decreased suggests hemolysis; increased suggests underproduction of red cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — decreased suggests underproduction, increased suggests a marrow response. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 59 (speaker notes)"},{"q":"Which four red cell indices does a complete blood count report?","choices":["Mean corpuscular volume, hemoglobin concentration, hematocrit percentage, and the red blood cell distribution width","Mean corpuscular volume, mean platelet volume, hematocrit, and reticulocyte count","Red blood cell count, hemoglobin, hematocrit, and mean corpuscular volume","Mean corpuscular volume, mean corpuscular hemoglobin, mean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration, and red blood cell distribution width"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — those four indices. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which four questions should be asked about any test?","choices":["What does it evaluate well, when do we order it, what are its strengths and limitations, and how do the results confirm the condition","What does it cost, how long does it take, who performs it, and where is it available","What is its sensitivity, what is its specificity, what is its predictive value, and what is its positive likelihood ratio","Is it invasive, is it painful, is it repeatable, and is it covered by insurance"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the four framing questions on the clinical applications slide. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Why do insulin and acid-base status matter specifically when reading diabetic ketoacidosis results?","choices":["Both shift potassium across the cell membrane, so the serum value can mislead","Both raise the measured potassium by causing haemolysis of the sample","Both reduce renal potassium excretion to near zero","Both interfere chemically with the potassium assay"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — both shift potassium across the cell membrane. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 10 (speaker notes)"},{"q":"Which of these produces hemoglobinuria?","choices":["A hemolytic transfusion reaction","A crush injury to the thigh","A bladder tumour","A ureteric stone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — rapid intravascular destruction of red cells. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman has two days of dysuria, frequency, and urgency with mild suprapubic tenderness. She is afebrile with no flank pain. What is the next step?","choices":["Serum creatinine and blood urea nitrogen alone","Renal ultrasound","Urinalysis","Computed tomography of the abdomen and pelvis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it is quick, non-invasive, and answers the question in front of you. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"On a chemistry panel, what does the abbreviation Cr stand for?","choices":["Chromium","Creatine kinase","C-reactive protein","Creatinine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — Reynolds singled this out as the one abbreviation that is not the element symbol it looks like. — Lecture recording, 26 August 2026"},{"q":"What should be done when a radiology report comes back vague?","choices":["Refer the patient to the relevant specialist","Contact the radiologist to discuss the patient","Repeat the study using a different modality","Order the next test in the diagnostic sequence"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the relationship is described as interprofessional and team-based. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"How long must urine sit in the bladder between voids for nitrites to be detectable?","choices":["About one hour","A full twenty-four hours","About thirty minutes","More than four hours"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the conversion needs that long. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which radiographic buzzword means a perforated bowel?","choices":["Air-fluid levels in dilated loops — small bowel obstruction","Coral-red fluorescence — erythrasma","Increased attenuation — a metallic foreign body","Free air under the diaphragm — perforated bowel"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — unless there has been recent laparoscopic surgery and the insufflated gas is not yet absorbed. — 2026-08-19 lecture recording, 1:22:50"},{"q":"During the potassium hydroxide procedure, how are the epithelial cells made visible?","choices":["Increase the illumination and open the iris diaphragm fully","Add a drop of methylene blue to the preparation","Warm the slide gently over a flame before reading","Reduce the illumination by lowering the condenser"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the step specified for the low-power examination. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What causes a false positive on the protein pad?","choices":["Contamination by genital secretions","Refrigeration of the specimen","A high fluid intake before collection","Reading the pad after ten minutes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — contamination is the recognised false positive. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Bence Jones proteins are suspected. Which test identifies them?","choices":["A reagent strip read by an automated reader","Urine protein electrophoresis","Urine culture and sensitivity","Microscopic examination of the sediment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — electrophoresis is what finds them. — Lecture recording, 1 September 2026, 40:09"},{"q":"How is the magnitude of a transaminase rise banded?","choices":["Mild under two times, moderate two to ten times, and severe over ten times the upper limit","Mild under ten times, moderate ten to fifty times, and severe over fifty times the upper limit","Mild under three times, moderate three to twenty times, and severe over twenty times the upper limit","Mild under five times, moderate five to fifteen times, and severe over fifteen times the upper limit"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — those three bands. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 17 (speaker notes)"},{"q":"What does incorporating prevalence into sensitivity produce?","choices":["The incidence of the condition","The pre-test probability applying to the next patient who is tested","The clinically useful positive predictive value, by way of Bayes' theorem","The specificity of the test in that population"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — moving from a test-centered probability to a patient-centered one. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 47"}]};
